window.QA_INDEX = {"docs":["Paper"],"sections":["1 Introduction","2 Related Work","3.1 Collection","3.2 Deduplication, Signal Checks, and Benchmark Screening","3.3 Clustering and Coverage-Aware Selection","3.4 Caption Quality and Production Supervision","4 Model, Training, and Evaluation","5 Scaling Analysis","6 Benchmark Results","7 Conclusion","A.1 Public Datasets and Web Audio","A.2 Audio Segmentation","A.3 Decoding and Signal Checks","A.4 Deduplication","A.5 Evaluation Quarantine","A.6 Storage Format","AI Use Statement","Abstract","Acknowledgments","B.1 Taxonomy Construction and Classifier Versions","B.2 Specialist Enrichment","B.3 Selection Parameters and Exact-Size Pass","C.1 Captioning Model","C.2 Metadata Prompt Expansion","D.1 Architecture Settings","D.2 Audio to Text Encoder Parameter Ratio","E Model Scaling","Ethics Statement","F Design Studies","G HEAR and MSEB Results","H Dataset Catalogue","Reproducibility Statement"],"chunks":[[0,17,1,"Recent advances in contrastive audio-language models can be attributed mainly to the scale and quality of their training data. Larger training datasets expose models to a broader range of sounds and corresponding audio captions. To exploit this, we collect 1.47 billion audio samples from academic datasets, internet datasets, web collections, Common Crawl, and YouTube, and process them with a multistage curation pipeline. Furthermore, we generate short and long captions for samples in our corpus that lack them.",0],[0,17,1,"We use our dataset to study the scaling behavior of contrastive audio-language models based on the NaFlex architecture. Our experiments identify trade-offs between model size and training data for a given compute budget. Moreover, our models outperform prior audio-text models of comparable size on the MAEB and HEAR benchmarks. As a result, our models can serve as a drop-in replacement for CLAP in downstream applications. We release our dataset, captions, model checkpoints, and code.",0],[0,0,1,"Recent advances in audio-language learning have shown that large-scale contrastive pretraining results in strong downstream performance [1, 2, 3]. In contrastive language-audio pretraining (CLAP), models learn to map audio recordings and their corresponding text descriptions into a shared embedding space. This facilitates tasks such as text-audio retrieval or audio classification. In addition to that, CLAP models support text-to-audio generation [4, 5] and audio source separation [6]. CLAP embeddings have also proven useful for data curation, including for detecting dataset overlap [7], filtering audio-text pairs [8, 9], selecting synthetic training examples [10], and refining audio captions [11].",0],[0,0,1,"CLAP models have improved as training datasets grow. LAION-CLAP used 633,526 audio-text pairs [2], M2D-CLAP used 2.6 million pairs [12], MS-CLAP23 increased this to 4.6 million pairs [13], and SLAP was trained with 109 million pairs [14]. These models report increasingly strong classification and retrieval results, while also differing in model size. As training datasets grow, choosing an appropriate model size becomes increasingly important for making the best use of available data and compute.",0],[0,0,1,"This question has been studied through scaling laws, which show that increasing both training data and model size generally improves downstream performance [15, 16]. In contrastive language-image pretraining (CLIP), scaling training data and model size has been a major source of these performance gains [17]. Recent work measured this in greater detail to compare models and datasets and estimate how many training samples to use for a given compute budget [18]. Moreover, data selection has been shown to be crucial [19].",0],[0,0,2,"The availability of large-scale audio-text data remains a bottleneck for studying the scaling of audio models. While vision and language pretraining have scaled to open, billion-scale web-scraped datasets with transparent curation pipelines [20, 19], public audio-text collections are orders of magnitude smaller. The largest open caption datasets for general audio reach about 50 million text-audio pairs [21, 8]. AF-Next used around 108 million samples from 1 million hours of audio [22], and MiDasheng trained on 1.1 million hours [23], both largely drawn from public datasets. We show that by combining public audio datasets with four other groups of sources, such as YouTube and audio derived from Common Crawl, this amount of data can be increased even more. In contrast, recent audio foundation models report training on private datasets of more than 100M hours, which are not publicly available [24, 25].",0],[0,0,2,"In this work, we collected LAION-Audio-XL, a training corpus of 365 million text-audio pairs from an initial collection of 1.47 billion audio samples drawn from public audio datasets, web data, Common Crawl, and YouTube (Figure 1). The dataset is processed with an extensive pipeline of multiple steps, including cleaning, deduplication, annotation, selection and captioning. From a raw pool of 1.47 billion audio samples, the final training corpus is a 365 million dataset of audio-text pairs, with audio of at most 30 seconds. We will release the indices, captions, metadata, and curation pipeline of this full training corpus to facilitate future research.",0],[0,0,2,"We use this corpus to train contrastive language-audio models. The audio encoder is a NaFlex audio transformer [26], which processes variable-length audio. The text encoder is loosely based on ModernBERT [27], but uses a SwiGLU + RMSNorm instead of GeGLU + LayerNorm. We train both encoders from scratch instead of initializing from a pretrained model. The model architecture scales more easily for testing and it allows for better display of scaling laws. Our experiments test 5 different sizes of models, from ≈27M to ≈340M parameters, and plot the results without any confounding effects from prior model weights.",0],[0,0,2,"For our 153M-parameter model, the average score on MAEB-30 [28] increases by 2.2 points when the seen samples are doubled between 1M and 1024M samples. Training only on audio of at most 30 seconds raises the score by 4.1 points and requires 3.5 times less compute than training on audio of up to 120 seconds. Our best model M reaches 52.3%, compared to 35.2% for LAION-CLAP and 44.1% for GLAP [29], the best other contrastive audio-text model we evaluated, with 5.6 times more parameters. The model is also the best model trained from scratch on the MAEB-30 leaderboard, where other models rely on backbones whose training data is not released. On HEAR [30], our S to L models score higher than all other audio-text models.",0],[0,0,2,"• We introduce a curated audio-text dataset with 365 million audio-text pairs, selected from 1.47 billion web and dataset audio samples through a fully documented pipeline. • We present a scaling study of contrastive audio-text training, showing a gain of ≈1.9 MAEB-30 percentage points per doubling of compute over four orders of magnitude.",0],[0,0,2,"• We train a 153M-parameter CLAP model that reaches 52.3% on MAEB-30, outperforming models of similar size such as M2D-CLAP (38.0%) and LAION-CLAP (35.2%).",0],[0,1,2,"Audio-text datasets. Earlier datasets such as Clotho [31] and AudioCaps [32] relied on a single source, often YouTube or Freesound. A recent trend shows that models are increasingly using combinations of sources [7]. Earlier examples of encoder models trained on a mixture of data include MS-CLAP trained on four and LAION-CLAP on 11 sources [3, 2]. Later, state-of-the-art models used 20+ sources (see Table 1 for an overview). The dataset presented in this work, LAION-Audio-XL, uses a combination of 450 sources, 336 of which are either part of or derived from academic work.",0],[0,1,2,"Other works enrich audio data with model generated captions, such as AudioSetCaps [11], Sound-VECaps [33], and ACAVCaps [34]. Some models also test varying captioning style, similar to our work, for example AudioVerse and ACAVCaps use a combination of long and short captions, including other variants [35, 34]. CapTalk and LP-MusicCaps do similar splits for speech and music, respectively [36, 37].",0],[0,1,3,"Figure 1: Our training dataset consists of many sources, spanning diverse sound categories, and our models achieve strong performance that improves with training scale. Left: the 365M training audio samples from source group to predicted sound category using our trained taxonomy classifier (see Section 3.3). Ribbon width is proportional to audio samples. Middle: MAEB [28] of our S model, LAION-Larger-CLAP [2] and MS-CLAP-23 [13]. Dotted: best of ten models (<500M parameters). Right: Scaling MAEB-30 against samples seen.",1],[0,1,3,"Table 1: LAION-Audio-XL compared to existing audio-text datasets, including standalone dataset releases and the training data of audio encoders and audio-language models. Our LAION-Audio-XL dataset is sourced from a range of online platforms and websites, and from existing data collections, and datasets (details are provided in Appendix H). ^{†}Dasheng trains on audio only.",1],[0,1,3,"Audio scaling laws. Researchers often release audio-language models in multiple sizes, where based on the scaling law properties, larger models tend to perform better than smaller models. Examples in audio encoder models include AuroLA [35] testing with audio-language LoRAs on 95M, 322M and 418M trainable parameters, and Dasheng consisting of 3 model sizes, 86M, 0.6B and 1.2B parameters [42]. Similarly, in the audio-language learning field, models are often released in multiple sizes, for example Qwen2.5Omni with a 3B and 7B model and MOSS-Audio with a 4B and 8B model [44, 45]. While scaling model sizes, it is also important to take into account scaling the amount of data.",0],[0,1,3,"For audio-language models, SODA [46] shows that the compute-optimal data amount needed grows faster than the amount of training parameters. PE-AV [47] separately compares how to scale data and model sizes in audio-visual models. Tseng et al. [48] train both contrastive and captioning models in pairs in 0.4M, 1M, 4M, and 10M audio-language pairs, and show that on smaller scales having a pretrained model gains the most improvements, but on larger scale training from scratch helps the most. To the best of our knowledge, this work is the first to present how a fixed compute budget should be divided between model sizes and data samples seen for contrastive audio-language models.",0],[0,1,3,"Audio taxonomy. The Audioset ontology is a taxonomy based on 632 classes, from which the 527 labels of Audioset dataset originate. These 632 originated from a list of more than 3000 candidates [49]. SALT later proposed an extension to 734 sound labels [50]. Universal Category System is an initiative for standardization of a set of categories of sound, with the January 2024 version spanning 753 subcategories under 82 categories [51]. This work uses a taxonomy based on 4,416 nodes consisting of 11 root nodes, and a maximum depth of 5. This taxonomy is the basis of a classifier on the set of leaf nodes of 3,718 classes to cluster a raw pool of audio data.",0],[0,1,4,"Figure 2: Overview of the LAION-Audio-XL dataset curation pipeline. We collected 1.48B audio samples from online platforms, of which 1.47B entered deduplication. Removing silent audio, duplicates (including SemDeDup [52]), and evaluation data leaves 1.28B audio samples. We then selected 500M audio samples based on categorical taxonomy annotations. The same annotations were used to prompt Qwen3-Omni [53] and MOSS-Audio [45] to generate short and long captions for 473M audio samples without textual descriptions, resulting in 500M captioned audio samples in total (the 500M selection). The 365M audio samples that are up to 30 seconds long form LAION-Audio-XL.",1],[0,2,4,"For training contrastive language-audio models, large-scale and diverse datasets are required. For the training of the model presented in this paper, a collection is aggregated over public datasets, YouTube videos, Common Crawl, among others. The final dataset contains 336 academic datasets, each tied to a published paper, preprint, challenge, or other academic release (Appendix A). Most of these datasets include valid descriptions that are also reused in the final dataset, with even a fraction of the full data being human-annotated. Table 1 compares the final dataset LAION-Audio-XL with existing audio-text datasets, and Figure 2 lists the processing steps outlined in this section.",0],[0,2,4,"YouTube contains the majority of our dataset. All audio collected on YouTube originates from Creative Commons-licensed videos with explicit reuse permissions. This source produces 1.18B audio samples extracted from approximately 33.61M full videos. YouTube audio is extracted across the full duration of each video. These videos have a mean duration of 13.1 minutes. Each video can therefore contribute many audio samples. The audio is segmented at silence boundaries into audio samples between 5 and 120 seconds.",0],[0,2,4,"Common Crawl consists of many websites scraped from the open web [54]. A scraper extracts all mentions of audio files with valid audio extensions across all crawls through CC-MAIN-2026-08. The first extraction wave covered 35 crawl directories from 2021 to 2025 and found about 3.3 billion audio links, of which about 423M are unique. The text surrounding the audio on the website is used as a context for subsequent caption generation.",0],[0,3,4,"The pipeline begins with removal of all empty or partial audios, or audios that cannot be read or decoded. Audios are kept if they are between 0.5 and 120 seconds, with an extra check if the audio contains at least 0.5 seconds of audible content. Exact matching groups audio samples by audio duration and the MD5 hash of uncompressed PCM bytes. This step removes 151.1M audio samples.",0],[0,3,5,"Removing duplicate and silent recordings saves compute [55] and prevents performance degradation and lowers memorization of repeated items [56]. Near-duplicate deduplication target recordings with minor acoustic variations. Two complementary strategies are used: acoustic matching through signal-processing fingerprints directly after exact matching, and semantic matching through self-supervised audio embeddings after the signal gate. Acoustic matching uses Olaf fingerprints [57] and removes 1.2M audio samples. Semantic nearduplicates are found using SemDeDup [52] based on Dasheng embeddings [42]. This stage removes 18.2M audio samples. Appendix A.4 describes both implementations in more detail.",0],[0,3,5,"The same deduplication also runs against the evaluation data of MAEB [28], HEAR [30] and MSEB [58]. Source identifiers, exact PCM hashes, acoustic fingerprints and a neural fingerprint find evaluation audio in the corpus, and every match is verified on the waveform (Appendix A.5).",0],[0,4,5,"After filtering and deduplication, an annotation pipeline gathers information regarding the audio. These serve as filters in manual selection and as extra content for captioning models. Using the addition of this extra information during captioning helps improve the caption quality (see Appendix C.2). A prominent part of our filtering is based on a taxonomy that reduces common content while protecting rare predicted categories. A model is trained with a linear output layer of 3,718 leaf buckets to perform this classification. In addition to the taxonomy filtering, the metadata collection involves emotion classification, language, speaker age and gender, pitch, music genre and style tags, and species predictions.",0],[0,4,5,"The selector combines a binary validity gate, a retention fraction, a cluster-density factor, and a mixture factor. Retention floors protect rare predicted categories, languages, expressive emotions, and species, while cluster-density and mixture factors reduce common content. A deterministic hash determines which candidates are kept. Appendix B.3 gives the full calculation and thresholds.",0],[0,4,5,"The curated pool is 75.3% speech and 11.7% music by predicted label. After selection and exact-size trimming, 500M base rows are obtained. The final training corpus keeps the 364,614,472 selected audio samples of at most 30 seconds (Appendix F). This changes the mixture to 45.0% speech, 22.5% music, 16.5% designed sound effects, and 16.1% other classes.",0],[0,5,5,"Caption quality is evaluated with both model judgments and human listening. Seven captioning systems produced short and long descriptions for a shared sample. The two MOSS-Audio-8B configurations Thinking and Non-Thinking ranked highest [45], followed by Qwen3-Omni [53]. Appendix C reports the model comparison and human listening study.",0],[0,5,5,"The captioners QWEN3-OMNI-30B-A3B-INSTRUCT and MOSS-AUDIO-8B-THINKING [53, 45] both caption disjoint halves of the data with a short and a long description for each audio, with 12 and 28 words on average. In total, this stage captions 472,152,808 audio samples with both caption lengths (94.01% of the full 500M selection).",0],[0,6,5,"Model. Our models are trained from scratch. The audio tower is based on a NaFlex vision transformer [72, 26] over log-mel patches [73]. The NaViT implementation is based on the timm package [74]. The audio input (32 kHz) is converted to Mel spectrograms with 128 bins between 50 Hz and 16 kHz, and a 320-sample hop. Non-overlapping 16 × 16 patches give 50 tokens per second or eight tokens per 0.16 seconds. We use RoPE [75, 76] to keep the coordinates of each patch.",0],[0,6,5,"The transformer blocks use RMSNorm [77], SwiGLU [78] and LayerScale [79]. One register token is added, and attention pooling [80] followed by two linear layers with a GELU [81] activation between them produces the clip embedding. The text tower is a bidirectional transformer with RoPE, RMSNorm, SwiGLU, and query-key normalization [82]. It has a 50,260-entry byte-pair vocabulary [83] and a 256-token context. The trained models span five sizes, between 27.3M (XXS) and 340.1M trainable parameters (Table 3). Smaller models enable efficient data filtering.",0],[0,6,5,"Training uses a standard CLIP objective on audio and text [84, 1]. The learnable logit scale is initialized to 1/0.07 and has an upper bound of 300. Audio is assigned to a batch of nearest length, with buckets of 6.88, 8.96, 12.96, 22.56, and 30.08 seconds, to reduce padding and speed up training. A GPU batch holds audio samples of one bucket under a budget of 49,152 audio tokens, so a 6.88 second short-clip batch has 136 audio samples and a 30 second batch has 32 per GPU (rounded down to be divisible by eight). Bucket probabilities are set so every bucket supplies about 20% of the samples, which corresponds to the training distribution. Audio samples are padded and masked inside their bucket.",0],[0,6,6,"Table 2: MAEB benchmark results. Parameter counts (Params) are totals over both towers, including embeddings. Results for our models use the last checkpoint of each size’s WSD v3 run: 1024M samples for XXS, XS, S, M and 512M samples for L, evaluated with un-normalised audio embeddings and with all-caps text inputs lowercased. We report the mean performance over 30 tasks, domain-specific (18 speech, 6 music, 6 sound tasks), and task-specific results: classification (12, including one multilabel), zero-shot classification (2), pair classification (3), clustering (3), and retrieval (10, including one reranking task). All scores are in %. Models are grouped by size, the best value in each column within a group is bolded.",1],[0,6,6,"All runs use AdamW [85] with β = (0.9, 0.98), weight decay 0.2, warmup-stable [86, 87] or cosine [88] learning rate decay, gradient clipping at 1.0, and bfloat16 autocast. Peak learning rate, warmup, and batch size are set per data budget (see Appendix E for details). The caption is randomly short or long for those who have both. The training compute C is counted as three times the forward cost of both towers [15], adapted to the distribution mix of the dataset. Table 3 shows that around 80% of the computation is caused by the audio encoder, due to the quadratic cost of attention on longer inputs.",0],[0,6,6,"Evaluation: We evaluated our models on the following benchmarks. Massive Audio Embedding Benchmark (MAEB-30) [28] is a collection of benchmarks that covers 30 individual tasks across speech, music, and environmental sounds, and spans classification, retrieval, and clustering tasks. The score is the unweighted mean over tasks. A task with several subsets, such as the languages of FLEURS, first averages within task. We group the tasks into speech (18 benchmarks), music (6 benchmarks), and sound (6 benchmarks) (Table 11). Audio samples are truncated to 30 seconds for evaluation to match the length of our training samples.",0],[0,6,7,"In addition to MAEB-30, we evaluate our models on the Holistic Evaluation of Audio Representations benchmark (HEAR) [30]. It covers speech, music, and environmental sounds. Since this benchmark is designed for the evaluation of audio representations only, not for text-audio evaluations, we use it as a quality measure for our audio embeddings. Additionally, we evaluate on a third, more speech-oriented, Massive Sound Embedding Benchmark (MSEB) [58].",0],[0,7,7,"Before scaling, we fixed the training setup in ablations of our cosine decay model (Appendix F). Training only on audio of at most 30 seconds scores 4.1 MAEB-30 points higher than training on audio of up to 120 seconds while using 3.5 times less compute, and the caption length has no effect beyond run-to-run variation.",0],[0,7,7,"Table 3: Model tiers for scaling study: Hidden dimension d, parameter counts, and forward-pass compute per encoder. Audio parameters include the MAP pool and projection layers. Text parameters include the pool and output projection but exclude the 50,260 × dt token-embedding table. Total parameters refer to the full model size, including embeddings. GFLOPs are forward-pass FLOPs per clip at the training length distribution.",1],[0,7,7,"Setup: Our scaling study includes five model sizes ranging from 27.3M to 340.1M parameters (Table 3). We train these models on our 365M dataset, with samples-seen scales D = {1M, 2M, 4M, 8M, 16M, 32M, 64M, 128M, 256M, 512M, 1024M}, a total of 11 data scales. We do this by sampling the training batches with replacement within each pass over the complete training pool. We train our runs with a warmup–stable–decay learning rate scheduling, which allows separate cooldowns from a shared run [89]. To allow enough optimization steps for smaller data scales, we split the runs into one short (up to 32M samples seen) and one long (up to 1B samples seen) training run. For more details on the hyperparameters used for these runs, refer to Appendix E.",0],[0,7,7,"For the formulation of our scaling study, we follow Nezhurina et al. [18], adapting L = aC^{b}, with C being compute and L referring to the mean MAEB-30 error score or subset of its 30 benchmarks, based on small compute scale saturation (BC). This results in the following requirements for our scaling functions:",0],[0,7,7,"Scaling Results: We fit our experimental data to Eq. 2 as shown in Figure 3. Our architecture shows a clear scaling trend on our 365M audio-text dataset. The fitted curve through the Pareto frontier shows that with each doubling of compute, MAEB-30 error (1 − mean score) is reduced by ≈ 4% or ≈ +1.9% points at the largest budget. We also tested the quality of our scaling prediction by excluding all runs with C ≥ 10^{11} GFLOPs. We are able to accurately predict the five runs on the compute-Pareto-frontier with C ≥ 10^{11} GFLOPs (see Figure 10).",0],[0,7,7,"Figure 3 shows that the smallest XXS model size is the computationally optimal choice up to 256M seen samples. Only at the end do we see that other model sizes can compete with it. Dataset scaling holds up to at least 256M for our smallest model size.",0],[0,7,8,"Figure 3: Scaling behavior on our LAION-Audio-XL dataset. MAEB-30 error rate (1 − mean score) per compute C (GFLOPs). Left: the compute-Pareto-optimal runs (solid points) of all warmup-stable-decay and cosine learning-rate-scheduled runs, with our fitted scaling function and its 95% bootstrap CI (1000 resamples). With α = 0.0603, the error reduces by 4.1% for each doubling of compute (≈ +1.9 mean MAEB-30 points at the largest budget). Right: each model size, from two WSD runs per size with individual cooldown phases, and the compute-Pareto envelope. Models XXS, XS, and S show slight saturation, while larger models need more compute to match the performance of smaller ones.",1],[0,7,8,"When splitting the MAEB-30 error score into its three domains (speech, music, and sounds) and examining their scaling behavior individually, we observe significant differences among them (Figure 4). Although sound-related benchmarks saturate after 1.5 × 10^{10} GFLOPs, speech and music do not reach the compute range tested with Climit = 5.5 × 10^{11}. We observe that there is only little improvement on speech benchmarks below ≈ 10^{9} when scaling compute, while no such warmup is needed for music and sounds (Figure 4).",0],[0,7,8,"The following section discusses the benchmark results of the last checkpoints in our scaling study for all model sizes. Model XXS, XS, S, M were trained on 1,024M data samples while model L was only trained on 512M data samples, making it comparable to the compute used for the best model M. We compare all our models to the leading models on the MAEB-30 leaderboard while distinguishing between smaller models below 1B parameters and larger models far surpassing our size with more than 1B parameters. Table 2 shows this in terms of mean MAEB benchmark score and split into domain and task type for each benchmark.",0],[0,8,8,"Our models perform strongly across all MAEB domains. Looking just at the mean MAEB performance, we see that our models surpass others several times their size. Figure 5 plots the mean MAEB-30 and HEAR scores against model size. On both benchmarks, our models score higher than Figure 4: Domain-specific scaling on our 365M other audio-text models of similar size. dataset. MAEB-30 error rate (1 − mean score) per Especially our smaller models show ex- compute C (GFLOPs) per model size and domain.",0],[0,8,9,"Figure 5: Embedding model performance by size. Average score across the individual tasks of MAEB (all 30 tasks) and HEAR (17 tasks). Blue squares are our own runs. Filled circles represent either audio-text multimodal models, and unfilled ones are audio-only models with no text component.",1],[0,8,9,"traordinary performance for their parameter count. Models XXS–M are therefore only beaten by models with at least 8× more parameters on MAEB-30. With a mean MAEB-30 score of 52.3% model M scores fourth compared on the official leader board, rivalling multi-modal foundation models with billions of parameters.",0],[0,8,9,"We achieve strong performance on speech tasks while beating the best model on music with three of our models (Table 2). Our training data holds much more speech than any other domain. Rebalancing it evenly between speech, music, and other classes lowers the MAEB-30 score at 256M seen samples from 43.0% to 41.7% (Appendix F). The original data distribution is therefore kept. On a per benchmark task breakdown, our models perform well on all of them except clustering which is known to misalign with other benchmarks as seen by the scores from other top-performing models [28]. For the individual results for all 30 MAEB benchmarks, refer to Table 11 in the Appendix.",0],[0,8,9,"The appendix reports two additional benchmarks. On HEAR (Table 13), our S, M and L models score between 81.4 to 82.0, higher than all other audio-text models (at most 80.7). Audio-only encoders such as USAD 2.0 [90] score up to 86.8. On MSEB (Table 14), our S and L models score 29.8 and 29.7, second only to M2D-CLAP (30.7) and higher than all multimodal models with billions of parameters (at most 27.9).",0],[0,9,9,"Our LAION-Audio-XL dataset shows that public datasets and web audio can support contrastive audio-language learning at scale with large-scale collection, filtering, deduplication, and captioning pipelines. Our focus on smaller models is backed by the observation that it is computationally more effective to train smaller models on more samples than to increase the parameter count. Training on audio of at most 30 seconds also produces a better model with less than one-third of the compute.",0],[0,9,9,"Downstream performance follows a fitted scaling trend, with continued gains in speech and music. These results motivate further data scaling. In particular, training larger models on more than 1B samples would show whether the scaling trend continues. The planned release of dataset indices, captions, model checkpoints, and code will allow other researchers to build on both the corpus and the models. Our CLAP models can replace other CLAP models and are efficient to run, making them especially useful for data curation and text-to-audio generation.",0],[0,27,10,"The dataset introduced in this work includes speech, music, public sound datasets, and web recordings with varying source terms. Almost all data used in this work is licensed under Creative Commons. Our release consists of source URLs for the audio data, metadata, captions, model checkpoints, and code. It does not redistribute audio.",0],[0,31,10,"This work reports the data collection and filtering steps, captioning protocols, model configurations, and code. Appendix H summarizes the stored audio samples, and separates sources that have a citable publication from sources that do not. The selection follows from the frozen configuration in Appendix B.3 and hashed clip identifiers. Figures are built from frozen count tables and pertask result files. We will release clip metadata, with download instructions, filenames and indices, metadata, captions, model checkpoints, training configurations, and the code.",0],[0,16,10,"In this work, we used generative AI tools for coding the analysis, for building figures, and for polishing the writing of this paper. Generative models were also part of the method. Two audio-language models were used for captioning the audio corpus, and a language model assigned captions to taxonomy leaves when the taxonomy and its classifier were built. Moreover, an audio foundation model judged caption quality. The paper describes these uses, their evaluation, and their measured errors. We reviewed all AI-assisted work. We did not use generative AI tools to formulate research questions or hypotheses. We take responsibility for the final content of this work, including any text produced with the aid of generative AI.",0],[0,18,10,"This work was in part supported by the BMFTR (FKZ: 16IS24060), and the DFG (SFB 1233, project number: 276693517). Jenia Jitsev and Marianna Nezhurina further acknowledge funding from the European Union Horizon Programme under grant no. 101214398 (ELLIOT), co-funding from the Digital Europe Programme under grant no. 101195233 (openEuroLLM) and grant no. 101198470 (LLMs4EU), funding from the EuroHPC Joint Undertaking under grant no. 101182737 (MIN-ERVA), funding by the German Federal Ministry of Research, Technology and Space (BMFTR) under grant no. 01IS24085C (OPENHAFM), and co-funding under the grant 16HPC117K (MIN-ERVA).",0],[0,18,10,"We gratefully acknowledge the Gauss Centre for Supercomputing e.V. (GCS) for providing computing resources through the John von Neumann Institute for Computing (NIC) on the JUWELS Booster and JUPITER supercomputers at the Jülich Supercomputing Centre (JSC). We also acknowledge storage resources on JUST operated by JSC and supported by the Helmholtz Data Federation (HDF), computing time granted by JARA and JSC on the JURECA supercomputer, access through the JUREAP (JUPITER Early Access Program), and computing time provided through the Gauss AI Competition (reformo) on JUPITER via GCS and BMFTR.",0],[0,18,10,"LAION further acknowledges a public storage grant from HuggingFace, enabling broad community access to open-source research outputs through HuggingFace repositories. We also thank the teams operating the supercomputing facilities at JSC for their support, especially Björn Hagemeier for assistance with data storage and handling.",0],[0,10,44,"For public datasets, a search across platforms such as Hugging Face, Zenodo, and the literature focused on gathering a large number of permissively licensed collections. This work invests in reusing existing academic datasets. These datasets often bring cleaner audio and have been tested in practice.",0],[0,10,44,"Academic datasets form the largest group by number of sources, accounting for 336 of the 450 sources listed in Table 16. YouTube and Common Crawl contribute most of the data by both clip count and audio duration. Table 16 displays these datasets. A further 40 sources omitted in that table contribute between one and nine captioned training audio samples each. These audio samples remain in the training corpus but are not listed in the catalogue to keep the catalogue more succinct.",0],[0,10,44,"YouTube audio is extracted across the full duration of each video. These videos have a mean duration of 13.1 minutes. Each video can therefore contribute many audio samples.",0],[0,10,45,"Common Crawl, a non-profit that maintains a free, open repository of web crawl data, releases multiple crawls per year, often containing roughly 2–3 billion web pages each. These crawls consist of WARC response records, which preserve the captured HTML, including the text surrounding audio links. Audio elements and direct audio-file links are extracted from this HTML, together with the page URL and up to five sentences before and five sentences after each link. When an audio element offers multiple sources, one is chosen by a fixed format preference that prioritizes WAV, FLAC, and AIFF.",0],[0,10,45,"The audio URLs are deduplicated and then downloaded, after which the audio is decoded and its duration checked. The first extraction wave covered 35 crawl directories from 2021 to 2025 and found about 3.3 billion audio links, of which about 423M are unique. A second wave added crawls from 2013 to 2020 and from mid-2025 to early 2026. Candidate URLs are not successful downloads: on a sample of 5.4M URLs, 54% downloaded and 44% of the downloads converted to audio, a net return of 23.7%.",0],[0,10,45,"Files larger than 50 MB were discarded before download to prioritize shorter recordings and accelerate retrieval. Audio that took longer than 30 seconds to download were ignored. Websites that did not connect or send any packet back to the scraper within 5 seconds were skipped, after a retry 2 seconds later. Files with a reported duration above 120 seconds were rejected before conversion. Downloaded recordings that still decoded to more than 120 seconds are subsequently segmented as described in Appendix A.2. The deduplication input includes 141,523,038 Common Crawl audio samples, of which 51,453,212 remain after exact and fingerprint deduplication.",0],[0,11,45,"An experiment compared three ways of cutting long recordings into clips: random crops, quality-filtered crops, and silence cuts (Figure 6). Each method used approximately 1M clips from the same YouTube source pool, captioned with Qwen3-Omni, to train our XS model with three seeds per method and approximately 8M samples seen per run. Silence cuts scored highest on MAEB-30 with 32.57 ± 0.18, followed by quality-filtered crops with 32.33±0.09 and random crops with 32.18±0.39 (mean ± standard deviation). All long recordings are therefore segmented with silence cuts, which only need CPU frame-energy scans of 8 kHz audio.",0],[0,11,45,"Figure 6: Segmentation comparison after 8M samples per run. Diamonds and whiskers show the MAEB-30 mean and standard deviation over three seeds. Open circles show individual runs. Time: seconds from reading the source audio to written FLAC clips, per hour of written clips, on the same 227 hours of YouTube audio Silence cuts occur below −40 dB lasting at least 1 second, at the midpoints of these regions. Audio samples shorter than 5 seconds merge with adjacent segments, targeting durations near 30 seconds. Segments longer than 120 seconds split at quiet frames within ten seconds of 30-second target positions. Beyond YouTube, another pass over the other data converted 3.04M long recordings into 7.15M segments.",1],[0,12,45,"The pipeline removes audios that libraries such as FFmpeg and soundfile are unable to read or decode. It also records 40 statistics per clip: decoded duration, original sample rate and channels, RMS and peak level, dynamic range, DC offset, the proportion and position of silent frames at −60 dB, clipping proportion, bandwidth, spectral centroid and flatness, band energies, and the three 10-second windows with most energy.",0],[0,13,45,"Exact identity. All data are converted to 16 kHz mono signed 16-bit PCM signal for exact-match representation. The key combines the MD5 hash of its bytes with the audio length. This checksum is used only for full exact matching. Audios that have the same semantic content, but are in different encodings or formats, will not match exactly. Exact matching of MD5 hash removes 151,056,336 in total. Common Crawl keeps 51.5M of its 141.5M audio samples after this stage. A small number of repeated files dominate these duplicates: ten files account for 73.6M removals, led by an audio containing a nudge to log in, downloaded 15.7M times under different URLs. The raw collection also contained 16.3M audio samples of pure silence.",0],[0,13,46,"Acoustic fingerprint matching. The acoustic stage links copies of the same recording after reencoding, level changes, cropping, or padding. It uses Olaf [57], a fingerprint technique similar to that used in Shazam. A fingerprint encodes the geometry of three spectral peaks, and two clips match when many fingerprints agree on one time offset. Fingerprints are computed on the full clip at 16 kHz mono, using a 64 ms Hamming window and an 8 ms hop. The extractor is a Rust port of Olaf’s C code with bit-identical hashes. Hashes exclude absolute time but include absolute frequency, so pitch-shifted or speed-changed copies are not matched.",0],[0,13,46,"Each shared hash votes for the offset between its occurrences, quantized to 32 ms. The match count m counts distinct shared hashes at the offset with most votes. Chance collisions between unrelated audio spread over many offsets. For audio with na and nb fingerprints, the score is and pairs with q ≥ 0.50 are linked. Audio with equal fingerprint counts must share at least half their fingerprints at one offset. A fully matching shorter clip must contain at least a quarter of the longer clip’s fingerprints. This limits matches caused by short shared fragments.",0],[0,13,46,"Matching uses a distributed hash join over 100B+ fingerprint rows from 1.47 billion audio. Rows are partitioned by hash into 512 buckets and joined on shared hashes, aggregated by clip pair and offset. Counts are summed across buckets before choosing each pair’s best offset. Hashes occurring more than 500 times within one source track are removed from that track before the join. This limits the quadratic join cost of frequent hashes.",0],[0,13,46,"Duplicate groups are connected components of edges with q ≥ 0.50. Edges touching audio already removed by exact matching are skipped. One audio per group is kept, preferring public datasets with or without captions first. When audio came from the same dataset group, it prefers audio without model-generated captions, then longer text, more channels, a higher sample rate, and the lowest identifier. This stage removes 1,196,096 audio beyond exact matching.",0],[0,13,46,"Semantic near-duplicate screening. The semantic deduplication step is there to remove duplicates that the fingerprint stage misses. It follows SemDeDup [52]: embed every clip, cluster the embeddings with k-means, and compare pairs only inside a cluster. The encoder is Dasheng-base [42], a masked audio encoder trained without text. Because this encoder is separate from the CLAP model, the deduplication does not depend on the representations of the CLAP model being trained.",0],[0,13,46,"In a small sample of 735K audio samples, taken from tar files that contain many similar recordings, 30,569 pairs reached the threshold. None had been removed by the earlier stages, and only 4.6% had any fingerprint edge (14.5% among pairs of audio samples of at least 2 seconds). Most new pairs were very short audio samples such as wake words and single utterances. Other cases were one file crawled twice and a clip next to its own re-segmented copy.",0],[0,13,46,"Each clip is decoded to 16 kHz mono, cut to its first 10 seconds where audio after 10 seconds is not compared in this step. Audio is zero-padded if shorter than 10 seconds. Dasheng-base returns a sequence of 768-dimensional frame features, which is mean pooled over time and stored in 16-bit floating point. Embeddings exist for 1,412,277,595 stored audio samples. The screen runs on the 1,263,840,901 pool audio samples of at least 2 seconds. Shorter audio samples are not compared.",0],[0,13,46,"The embeddings are normalized to unit length and clustered with k-means in faiss on one GPU with k = 65,536, 20 iterations, a training sample of 20M random audio samples, seed 0, and assignment of every clip to its nearest centroid. The update is the standard Euclidean one. Centroids are not re-normalized. A target of one cluster per 2,000 audio samples would need about 632,000 clusters, and assignment cost grows with Nk, so k was capped at 65,536. The median cluster has 18,226 audio samples and the largest 95,813. The cluster sizes of this run are the density signal nk(i) in Equation 4. At the selector cap of 2^{14.5}, 17,870 clusters exceed the cap and hold 42.6% of the audio samples.",0],[0,13,46,"Inside each cluster, the pairwise cosine matrix of the members is computed on the GPU. This cosine similarity is set relatively high, as earlier results show false-positives. Mostly only pairs at 0.995 and above in the listening sets were repeated recordings. Clusters with more than 20,000 members are subsampled to 20,000 random members before the comparison. Within each k-means cluster, near-deduplicate samples with >0.995 cosine similarity form groups, where only 1 sample per group is kept. The keep order is by group: public datasets with captions are more important than noisy YouTube data. The run reported 113,572,831 removal decisions (9.0% of the clustered audio samples). Only 18,162,786 were applied.",0],[0,14,47,"All audio in the 500M selection and LAION-Audio-XL is screened against the evaluation data of MAEB, HEAR and MSEB and against the AudioCaps, Clotho and VGGSound test sets, 46 datasets in total. The screen uses the three matching families of the corpus deduplication together with identifier matching. Identifier matching compares source identifiers, file names, Freesound and YouTube identifiers with their time windows, and test-split metadata with the benchmark records. Exact matching compares MD5 hashes of decoded 16 kHz mono PCM. Acoustic matching uses two signal fingerprints, spectral-peak landmarks as in Olaf and binary band-energy sub-fingerprints, which find copies that were re-encoded, trimmed or embedded in longer recordings.",0],[0,14,47,"Semantic matching uses a neural fingerprint of 1-second windows, which also covers codec and mixing changes. An exhaustive search of every evaluation item over the corpus waveforms confirms each match with a test of statistical significance, and every candidate pair is verified on the waveform by cross-correlation. An audio sample is removed when it holds the same recording as an evaluation item or an excerpt of it. Short generic sounds that match by chance are kept.",0],[0,15,47,"The 500M selection and LAION-Audio-XL use WebDataset shards pairing FLAC audio with JSON metadata under a shared key, with numeric archive names and at most 4,096 samples per shard. Audio was initially normalized to 48 kHz and subsequently recoded to 32 kHz, reducing storage by approximately 16%. This choice was supported by measurements showing that 99.68% of samples contained at least 95% of their spectral energy below 13 kHz. The final dataset uses approximately 417 TB.",0],[0,19,47,"The taxonomy was expanded through caption-classification batches. Claude Sonnet 4.5 [91] assigned each caption to a leaf and proposed additional categories. Merging removed duplicates and rejected also categories consisting of multiple events. The final hierarchy contains 4,416 nodes, including 4,085 leaves and 11 top-level groups, with depth up to five. The top-level groups are speech, music, designed sound effects, urban/transport, animal/bioacoustics, human nonspeech, nature/environmental, domestic/household, interface/electronic, sports/recreation, and industrial/mechanical. For the classifier, only depths up to 3 were kept (instead of the full hierarchy of 5), resulting in a smaller prediction vocabulary of 3,718.",0],[0,19,47,"The original taxonomy classifier is a multilayer perceptron (MLP) trained on 1,280-dimensional mean-pooled features from a frozen Dasheng-0.6B encoder. A shared 1,024-unit hidden layer applies a linear transformation, batch normalization, GELU activation, and dropout. Separate linear output layers map the hidden representation to either leaf or top-level or second-level class logits. The labelled set has 232,728 caption-distilled audio samples, split by archive into 185,006 training, 22,404 validation, and 25,318 test audio samples. The MLP classifier is optimized for 40 epochs with AdamW, batch size 8,192, learning rate 10^{−3}, and weight decay 10^{−4}. A later improvement used a Qwen2.5-Omni [44] model to relabel the training set, which improved top-level mapping accuracy on the held-out test set of 18,848 samples from 75.2% to 85.6%. The leaf-derived top-level mapping accuracy improved from 77.4% to 86.4%.",0],[0,20,47,"Before our selection process, we gather extra data to make a better informed decision. Specialist enrichment is routed by the predicted top-level taxonomy class. Speech samples receive language, emotion, speaker age and gender, and pitch predictions. Music samples receive genre and style tags, tempo, and musical key. Animal samples receive bioacoustic class predictions. These outputs accompany the taxonomy labels and signal statistics, including duration, loudness, silence fraction, clipping fraction, and bandwidth.",0],[0,20,48,"Figure 7: Visualization of the sound taxonomy. The five levels contain 11, 837, 3,150, 393, and 25 nodes, respectively, for a total of 4,416 nodes, including 4,085 leaves. Coloured inner arcs denote the 11 top-level groups, ordered clockwise from speech at the top. Branches show 77 of the 837 second-level categories, selected by descendant leaf count and audio sample count (grey numbers, respectively). Italic rows summarize omitted categories and their leaves.",1],[0,20,48,"For speech, MMS-LID-4017 [92] predicts one of 4,017 language labels, using the highest-scoring label and its probability. Emotion2vec+ large [93] predicts one of nine labels: angry, disgusted, fearful, happy, neutral, other, sad, surprised, and unknown. The wav2vec2 [94] age and gender model [95] provides a continuous age estimate in years and a label of either female, male, or child. CREPE [96] estimates the pitch of the voice (its fundamental frequency) between 50 and 550 Hz every 200 ms. The stored pitch is the median of these estimates, counting only the moments where the voice is clearly periodic (periodicity above 0.5).",0],[0,20,48,"For music, MAEST [97] predicts out of 400 Discogs genre and style labels. Signal processing with librosa estimates continuous tempo in beats per minute and one of 24 musical keys, of 12 pitch classes in major or minor mode. For bioacoustics, Perch 2.0 [98] has 14,795 output classes, consisting of 14,597 species labels and 198 general sound-event classes. The five highest-scoring classes and their logits are used.",0],[0,21,49,"Here, nk(i) is the number of clips in the acoustic cluster. The factor ci reduces the selection probability for clips in large clusters. For the 500M selection, each clip must pass all of the following checks: the audio must decode successfully, clip must contain at least 1 s of content and its original duration must be between 0.5 and 120 s. Clips with both silence fraction ≥ 0.98 and RMS ≤ −80 dB are excluded. We set gi = 1 if the clip passes all checks, and gi = 0 otherwise. For these correct clips, the rules below set a minimum selection probability. The value ri is the largest probability assigned by these rules, or zero if none applies. A value of 1 means the clip is always kept.",0],[0,21,49,"For each category at the most specific level of the taxonomy, valid candidates are sorted from highest to lowest prediction confidence. Each receives a weight of 1 at confidence ≥ 0.5, or 0.3 otherwise. Starting at the top of the list, we add these weights until their sum reaches or exceeds 2,000. Each clip included in this sum gets its weight as a minimum selection probability. If the sum for the whole list is below 2,000, this applies to all clips in the list.",0],[0,21,49,"We set ri = 1 for up to 50,000 valid candidates for each predicted language, chosen from highest to lowest language confidence. The same applies to up to 1M candidates per emotion label and 500 per species label, chosen from lowest to highest hash value. The emotion labels covered by this rule are angry, disgusted, fearful, happy, sad, and surprised. We also set ri = 1 for valid clips whose second top-level prediction has probability ≥ 0.25 for music and designed sound effects, or ≥ 0.10 for the remaining categories other than speech. This rule also covers valid clips from CommonVoice_22_0 and EarSAVAS, to promote speaker and language diversity.",0],[0,21,49,"The target class proportions are applied to expected clip counts, where a candidate selected with probability 0.3 contributes 0.3 to the count. The initial budget is T = 660M before the final size adjustment, with shares (0.37, 0.29, 0.34) for speech, music, and all remaining classes ((244.2, 191.4, 224.4)M). Unused budget in one group is not moved to another. For second-level category h, the protected expected count Ph comes from the minimum probabilities ri. The adjustable expected count Dh is the additional contribution after applying the cluster-size factor ci, before adjusting the class proportions:",0],[0,21,49,"where (x)+ = max(x, 0) and Xh is the reduction in expected count for category h. If a further reduction is needed, the same calculation is applied to the categories left out of the first step. The protected expected count Ph is never reduced. The implementation skips reductions of at most 0.5 in expected clip count.",0],[0,21,49,"Within each category requiring a reduction, candidates with a positive adjustable contribution di = gi(1 − ri)ci are sorted by decreasing removal score Categories requiring no reduction have wi = 1. We hash each clip’s audio ID with a fixed seed and scale the result to a value between 0 and 1. The clip is selected if this value is below its selection probability pi. Using a fixed seed makes this step reproducible.",0],[0,21,50,"Table 4: Caption Model Comparison. Arrows indicate whether higher (↑) or lower (↓) is better. Speed: Throughput is given in samples/s per node (4x GH200 GPUs). Diversity: Vocab@80k and vocab@400k are the number of unique words across 80k short and 400k long caption tokens, respectively. Quality: Human rank and LLM rank (Gemini 3.6 Flash) report the mean rank among the 7 captioners for the 50 shared audio samples (1 = best, 7 = worst) ± 95% CI. LLM BT is the Bradley–Terry strength for the Gemini 3.6 Flash judge on the full 8997 test audio samples.",1],[0,21,50,"Within each category requiring a reduction, candidates with a positive adjustable contribution di = gi(1 − ri)ci are sorted by decreasing removal score Categories requiring no reduction have wi = 1. We hash each clip’s audio ID with a fixed seed and scale the result to a value between 0 and 1. The clip is selected if this value is below its selection probability pi. Using a fixed seed makes this step reproducible.",0],[0,21,50,"The selected clips are matched to their current archive locations, and duplicate entries with the same archive path and key are removed. Selected clips not covered by any minimum probability rule above are labeled fill. The final size adjustment keeps all non-speech clips and all clips covered by these rules. Among speech clips labeled fill, it keeps those with the lowest deterministic hashes until the base index has exactly 500,000,000 rows. Hash values are grouped into ranges, and the entries in the range containing the cutoff are sorted to get the exact count.",0],[0,21,50,"Speech falls to 42.7%, music accounts for 27.4%, and the remaining classes account for 30.0%. The same validated additions used for all selection variants are then added. The final index labels record the selection rule for each clip: 72.307% are fill, 22.577% are assigned to the secondary-prediction or source-dataset rules, and the rest to the category, language, emotion, or species rules, or to additions. The secondary-prediction and source-dataset rules account for more selected audio samples than any one of the other minimum probability rules.",0],[0,22,50,"Captioning a large training pool is a one-shot, expensive decision. When generating captions for a large dataset, we want to maximize their quality and diversity while keeping compute costs low. If the captions often contain incorrect details or are very similar to one another, we end up with a weak training signal. The biggest influence on these three factors comes from the large audio-language models used for captioning. To pick the best models for our use case, we tested seven configurations covering six model candidates: MOSS-AUDIO-8B (thinking on and off) [45], QWEN3-OMNI-30B-A3B-INSTRUCT [53], QWEN2.5-OMNI-7B [44], AUDIOFLAMINGONEXT [22], NEMOTRON-3-NANO-OMNI-30B-A3B-REASONING-FP8 [99], and NEMOTRON-LABS-AUDEX-30B-A3B [100]. Model names in the following might be shortened.",0],[0,22,51,"Quality: In the following study (Table 4), we compare the caption quality of the candidate models. We use an automated LLM review in which all model captions are ranked against one another for each clip. Gemini 3.6 Flash [101] ranked anonymized and shuffled candidates according to faithfulness, coverage, and specificity. Additional permutations, pairwise comparisons, and a claim audit measured judge consistency. We picked a subset of 8997 audio samples matching our data distribution. After generating one caption per candidate, we calculate the Bradley–Terry strength [102] from the automated rankings. Table 4 shows that MOSS Audio 8B produces the best results by showing the highest Bradley–Terry strength in the LLM review regardless of the thinking setting. In contrast, Audex and Nemotron-3 are the two weakest models across the evaluation.",0],[0,22,51,"To ground these results in human perception, we rank a subset of 50 audio samples (25 short, 25 long captions) with two human annotators (“human rank”) and compare the results with the LLM judge (“LLM rank”). The labeling interface anonymizes models and shuffles the captions. The annotators only agreed 12 out of 50 times on their top choice, with a mean per-clip Kendall τb of +0.295 [+0.202, +0.387]. Compared to the inter-annotator agreement, the LLM judge falls within the range of a human rater: τb = +0.345 [+0.250, +0.436] with one annotator and +0.265 [+0.166, +0.362] with the other.",0],[0,22,51,"Brackets are 95% percentile bootstrap confidence intervals from 20,000 resamples over clips. Pooling the two annotators, humans and judge produce nearly the same ordering of the seven captioners (Table 4). Both put the two MOSS Audio configurations on top and Nemotron-3-Nano-Omni and Audex-30B-A3B at the bottom. The largest single disagreement is Qwen3-Omni-30B-A3B, which the judge places 3rd while our human ranking places it 5th.",0],[0,22,51,"Note that the set of n = 50 human-annotated audio samples is meant to validate the LLM review, not to serve as a leaderboard. It can only give a rough impression of the top and bottom models while checking that the LLM judge behaves similarly to a human rater.",0],[0,22,51,"Speed: Another important aspect when selecting captioning models is inference speed. When captioning several hundred million samples, even slightly slower models can lead to a significant increase in compute cost. To optimize the captioning speed of each model, we considered serving layout, hyperparameter settings (e.g., maximum number of sequences), NUMA bindings, caching, output format, and more. We ended up with large differences of up to ~5× between our fastest (Nemotron, 96.5 samples/s/node) and slowest (MOSS thinking-OFF, 19.2 samples/s/node) model (Table 4). This puts the theoretical compute estimate for 470 million audio samples between 1,350 and 6800 node-hours.",0],[0,22,51,"Diversity: Table 4 shows the number of unique words within a limited number of text tokens. We evaluate this metric on short and long captions with 80k and 400k token limits, respectively. This way, the metric does not depend on the verbosity of each model. There is a ~2.4× difference between the most repetitive (Nemotron-3-Nano-Omni and Qwen3-Omni-30B-A3B) and the most diverse (MOSS Audio think-ON) model.",0],[0,22,51,"Based on the results of this study and our three main criteria (quality, diversity, and computational cost), two models stood out to us. MOSS Audio think-ON with high diversity and quality, while being slow and Qwen3-Omni for its speed, while still performing strongly on the Bradley–Terry strength metric. We decided to use a combination of Qwen3-Omni and MOSS Audio think-ON. This combination achieves a caption diversity of ~4,395 and ~11,976 unique words per 80k/400k text tokens, respectively, while balancing the quality and compute cost of MOSS Audio think-ON with the speed of Qwen3-Omni-30B-A3B. Both models then captioned disjoint, randomly selected halves of the total ~470 million audio samples, each with a long and a short caption. This results in a theoretical compute cost of about 3,710 node-hours (4× GH200 per node).",0],[0,23,51,"When captioning audio samples, the prompt given to the captioning model directly affects the quality of the produced caption. In addition to the general instructions, we identified three primary approaches to include each sample’s metadata generated by specialist models (Appendix B.2). They are (1) passing only general instructions with the audio, (2) attaching the whole raw JSON metadata string, or (3) adding only selected JSON fields converted to natural language to the prompt.",0],[0,23,51,"We tested these three approaches in combination with our two captioning models (Qwen3-Omni and MOSS Audio) on 976 audio samples, selected to cover the dataset’s duration and domains, resulting in six long captions per clip. Gemini 3.6 Flash then ranked all 6 captions for the same audio sample by accuracy, completeness, temporal details, specificity, and writing. The judge received only the audio and the six randomly ordered captions, never the metadata, so it could not simply confirm our specialists’ predictions.",0],[0,23,52,"Table 5: Attaching raw metadata strings leads to the highest caption quality. Overall ranking of two captioning models with three prompt types across the same 976 audio samples, evaluated with Gemini 3.6 Flash (thinking: low). Mean rank ranges from 1 (best) to 6 (worst). Win rate refers to being the best caption for a clip (higher-is-better). Brackets give 95% percentile bootstrap confidence intervals over B = 20,000 resamples of the 976 audio samples.",1],[0,23,52,"Table 6: Model tiers for WSD scaling runs: Audio and text columns give width × depth, number of attention heads, and SwiGLU hidden size per encoder. A-Size and T-Size are the respective encoder parameter counts in millions. Audio counts include the MAP pool and projection layers. Text counts include the pool and output projection but exclude the 50,260 × dt token-embedding table. N (non-embedding) is the sum of A-Size and T-Size. GFLOPs are total (audio + text tower) forward-pass FLOPs per clip at the training length distribution.",1],[0,23,52,"The results in Table 5 show that adding the raw metadata JSON string results in the best average performance across models when ranking them. This observation also holds for almost all audio lengths and domains. We attribute this to the quality of the metadata and the ability of modern large audio-language models to process JSON strings well. Considering these results, we include the raw metadata JSON string in the prompts for our captioning process.",0],[0,24,52,"All models used in our experiments share a single dual-encoder layout. The layout is a contrastive audio–text model in the CLAP family: a spectrogram Vision Transformer with native variable-length input (NaFlex) [26] encodes audio, a bidirectional Transformer encodes text, and both are mapped into a shared embedding space trained with a standard CLIP objective on audio and text.",0],[0,24,52,"For the architectural design, we aimed to align with established encoder widths and depths while offering a broad range of sizes. We ended up with five model scales from XXS (27.3M parameters) up to L (340M parameters) that are aligned with known small-scale VIT dimensions [103] and optimized for hardware alignment as seen in Table 6. For more details on the audio-to-text encoder ratio, refer to Appendix D.2. With this setup, we cover the computational range per forward pass from an average of 26.1 GFLOPs up to 357.9 GFLOPs per clip, given our training distribution.",0],[0,24,52,"For the architectural hyperparameters and mechanisms, we adopt proven approaches such as RoPE and SwiGLU, while motivating others through our own experiments in Section F. An overview of key design choices can be found in Table 7.",0],[0,24,53,"Table 7: Architecture settings shared by all tiers in the scaling study. For details on architecture shape refer to Table 6 & 8.",1],[0,25,53,"A main component of the architecture design is the parameter ratio between the audio and text encoders. This ratio depends on many factors, including downstream benchmarks, model size, and the training corpus. In the following, all reported ratios and parameter shares exclude parameters related to text token embedding, which is a fixed lookup cost. When comparing other contrastive pre-trained architectures, one finds substantial discrepancies among them. While OpenCLIP [17] and SigLIP2 [26] allocate up to ~78% of the model parameters to the vision encoder, SigLIP1 [80] splits evenly, and LAION-CLAP [2] and MS-CLAP [3] do the opposite, by assigning between ~51% and ~73% of parameters to the text encoder.",0],[0,25,53,"The difference is that vision-based contrastive models are trained on large vision-language datasets that provide substantial visual variety while offering rather compressed text captions. Therefore, scaling the vision encoder results in higher benchmarks. Older CLAP models, on the other hand, struggle with overfitting due to data scarcity while depending on pre-trained component sizes.",0],[0,25,53,"We tested four different parameter allocations on different data scales and model sizes, as shown in Figure 8. Changing the audio-to-text ratio from 2.36:1 to 1:2 does not show significant differences in the MAEB mean score with D ≤ 64M. This observation does not hold for the two XS parameter ratios we tested on D = 512M, with a difference of 2.2 percentage points. Performance differences might appear as D increases further. For computational reasons, we stopped after the runs shown in Figure 8.",0],[0,25,53,"In general, shifting parameters to the text encoder results in faster inference speeds given that the encoder widths remain hardware-aligned. Across our model range, this results in up to ~22% faster inference speed. Considering these aspects, we chose conservative CLIP-inspired ratios by allocating ~51% to ~57% of parameters to the audio encoder. This increases inference speed compared to older LAION-CLAP and MS-CLAP ratios, while accounting for our new data scale and architectural design freedom by avoiding pre-trained components. For more details on the architecture, refer to Appendix D.1.",0],[0,26,53,"Setup and hyperparameters: All our scaling plots are based on two batches of runs. One is trained with warmup-stable-decay (WSD), and the other with cosine learning-rate scheduling. Both of these batches are consistent with each other but differ in the scheduler, warmup, learning rate, batch size in addition to altering the architecture proportions. While the older cosine-scheduled runs were done a bit earlier in development, they have a more audio-encoder-heavy parameter allocation and slightly change the total number of parameters (Table 8). We mark them with a C after the model size, like XS-C. Since we compare the error rate to computation time while varying hyperparameters like model size, we can include both in the scaling plots without issue.",0],[0,26,53,"For all other plots and tables, unless explicitly mentioned otherwise, we always refer to the warmup-stable-decay scheduled runs, as they almost always outperform the cosine runs. You can find the respective training settings for the WSD, and the cosine runs in Table 9 and 10 respectively. We did some initial learning rate and batch size experiments on 32M, 64M, and 128M data scales and extrapolated them to other scales following [18]. We then adapted the learning rate for WSD with a factor of ×0.75 and ×0.80 while choosing a data scale appropriate batch size.",0],[0,26,54,"Figure 8: Audio:text capacity choice does not significantly affect performance up to 64M samples. Left: parameter composition of the four capacity splits for model sizes XS and M. Within a tier, the non-embedding total is held constant. The 50k-vocab token embedding is adapted to the individual layer dimensions and is a fixed lookup cost, not encoder capacity. Right: Mean MAEB percentage scores over 30 tasks per training sample seen D show that the audio-to-text tower ratio does not have a significant effect up to 64M samples.",1],[0,26,54,"Table 8: Model tiers for cosine scheduled scaling runs. Audio and text columns give width × depth, number of attention heads, and SwiGLU hidden size per encoder. A-Size and T-Size are the respective encoder parameter counts in millions. Audio counts include the MAP pool and projection layers. Text counts include the pool and output projection but exclude the 50,260 × dt token-embedding table. N (non-embedding) is the model size used in all scaling fits while total refers to the actual model size. GFLOPs are total (audio + text tower) forward-pass FLOPs per clip at the training length distribution.",1],[0,26,54,"Additional scaling results: Figure 9 shows the scaling behavior of our model in contrast to different tasks of MAEB-30. It shows that, in addition to the mean MAEB-30 error rate, pair classification, retrieval, and zero-shot classification also scale well with compute. But what is not shown on the mean score scale is that classification tasks saturate at the end of our compute budget. Even more extreme are the clustering benchmarks which scale inversely to the compute. Given our equation 2 and its constraints, this leads to an almost flat line in the plot. This is a known challenge of the MAEB benchmark as noted in their conclusion [28].",0],[0,26,55,"Table 9: Training configuration of the cooled-down WSD endpoints. “XXS–L” denotes all five of our model sizes (XXS, XS, S, M, L); Samples is the number of training samples seen. Every model size shares the schedule at a given budget. Warmup and cooldown are in optimizer steps, and the cooldown decays the learning rate linearly to zero over the final 20 % of the budget. Batch size varies based on the length buckets.",1],[0,26,55,"Table 10: Training hyperparameters for the cosine learning rate scheduled scaling runs. “(XXS–L)-C” denotes all five of our model sizes (XXS–C, XS–C, S–C, M–C, L–C); Samples is the number of training samples seen. Batch size varies based on the length buckets",1],[0,26,55,"We also tested the quality of our scaling prediction by excluding all runs with C ≥ 10^{11} GFLOPs. As shown in Figure 10, we are able to accurately predict the five runs on the compute-Pareto-frontier with C ≥ 10^{11} GFLOPs. The predicted runs are marked with an × in the figure. Our predictions have a mean absolute error of 0.5 MAEB-30 points and a mean squared error of 5.22 × 10^{−5}.",0],[0,26,55,"Last but not least, we plot the compute against our validation loss. Our validation set consists of ≈ 1.5k samples that were not used in training. Figure 11 shows isolines with the same data scale, while marking the lowest loss with a star. From these valleys, we can determine the most sample efficient model size that is neither over- nor under-trained. An example for this would be D = 32M, where size M offers the best compromise between model size and data scale for minimizing the validation loss. To predict this for a larger D, we would need to train larger models with up to D = 1,024M since there is no valley for them yet. One important note is that validation loss does not directly transfer to downstream benchmark performance as shown in Figure 3.",0],[0,26,56,"Figure 10: Scaling predictions based on less than 10^{11} GFLOPs. We are able to accurately predict our five best performing frontier models with more than 10^{11} GFLOPs of compute. We can do this with a mean squared error of 5.22 × 10^{−5} or a mean absolute error of 5.1 × 10^{−3}.",1],[0,26,56,"Figure 9: Scaling behavior on the full MAEB benchmark and per task type. MAEB-30 error rate (1 - mean score) per compute C (GFLOPs). The band marks the 95% bootstrap CI with 1000 resamples. It shows clear trends in compute scaling for zero-shot (ZS) classification, pair classification, and retrieval. In contrast, it isolates the saturating classification task and the inversely scaled clustering benchmark subset.",1],[0,26,56,"Figure 11: Validation loss per compute C. We plot our models based on compute and data scale against their validation loss with batch size B = 256. We can see clear valleys in the loss at smaller compute scales, indicating the optimal compute for a given data scale. This plot only contains runs trained on a warmup-stable-decay learning rate schedule.",1],[0,26,57,"Table 11: MAEB-30 task scores (%) of the final scaling-grid models, each the largest cooled-down checkpoint that has finished training for its tier. Parameter counts are totals over both towers, including embeddings. All models are evaluated with native MTEB 2.21.0 [104] on the same embedding wrapper, with audio capped at 30 s (1504 tokens), un-normalised audio embeddings, and all-caps text inputs lowercased. Multi-subset tasks average all subsets. Bold marks the best value per row.",1],[0,26,57,"Music (6 tasks) JamAlt artist A2A JamAlt lyrics A2T Beijing Opera GTZAN genre Mridangam tonic GTZAN reranking Mean",0],[0,28,57,"Audio duration: Training on audio of at most 30 seconds produces a better model with less than one-third of the compute. At 256 million training samples seen, dropping longer audio samples scores 47.1%, cropping them to 30 seconds scores 46.5%, and training on audio up to 120 seconds scores only 43.0% on the mean MAEB benchmark (Table 12). For this evaluation clips were cut of at 120 seconds. Allowing batches to hold up to 120-second audio samples leads to far more audio tokens per clip. At 50 audio tokens per second, the maximum number of audio tokens increases from 1504 (30 seconds) to 6008 (120 seconds), leading to smaller batches per GPU and slower training.",0],[0,28,57,"The 120-second bucket holds only 8 audio samples per GPU batch but accounts for about three-quarters of the training FLOPs because attention cost grows with the square of the token count. Removing it reduces the cost per sample from 1.94 × 10^{12} to 0.56 × 10^{12} FLOPs, a factor of 3.5, and increases throughput on 128 GPUs from 6,600 to 12,300 samples per second. A compromise for this slowdown could be to oversample shorter buckets while keeping the 120-second bucket. As shown in Table 12, this oversampling approach does not produce better results with 41.9% mean MAEB score. We fix the policy that drops longer audio samples for the model-size grid. It leaves 364,614,472 of the 499,533,088 audio samples.",0],[0,28,58,"Table 12: Impact of training on different audio lengths, and caption types on mean MAEB-30 performance for our model of size M-C. Domain columns average the 18 speech, 6 music, and 6 sound tasks. All models were trained on the 500M selection with a 120 second audio length limit unless stated otherwise.",1],[0,28,58,"Caption ratio: Training on shorter or longer captions typically leads to performance trade-offs in downstream benchmarks. We tested to train only on the short or long caption provided by our dataset. We observed that no caption policy is better than another within run-to-run variation. At 128M samples the fixed per-clip mix of short and long captions scores 39.1%, short captions only 38.6%, and long captions only 38.5% on the MAEB benchmark (Table 12). Given these results and the fact that an even split offers the most caption diversity, we fix the default to an even split for further experiments.",0],[0,28,58,"Data domain: Our training dataset has significantly more speech data than any other domain. To test whether this is positive or negative, we tried balancing the dataset evenly between speech, music and others. We do this by dropping samples during loading, with probabilities of 0% for music, 40% for speech, and 4% for other classes. This keeps the data pool the same and oversamples to achieve a more even training data distribution. Training a model on 256 million rebalanced samples, scores 41.7% while reaching 43.0% on MAEB without it. We therefore preserve the dataset’s original data distribution for other experiments.",0],[0,29,59,"Table 13 reports the HEAR scores of Figure 5 by domain and task type. Table 14 reports the English tasks of the Massive Sound Embedding Benchmark (MSEB) [58] for most models of Table 2.",0],[0,29,59,"Table 13: HEAR benchmark results for the models of Table 2 and the audio-only encoders of Figure 5, with the same embeddings as there. We report the mean over 17 tasks (all HEAR tasks except Beehive and Maestro 5h), domain-specific means and the three HEAR task types: clip classification, multi-label tagging (FSD50K) and timestamp event detection (DCASE 2016). Audio-only models are evaluated by model’s authors or the official HEAR 2021 leaderboard. Audio-text models are evaluated by us. Within each group models are sorted by the mean, and only models with every value available are listed. For audio-only models, we retain the best one or two variants of each method by mean score All scores are in %. The best listed value in each column is bolded, separately for the audio-only models and for the audio-text models together with ours.",1],[0,29,60,"Table 14: MSEB benchmark results on the English tasks, for most models of Table 2 (same parameter counts and checkpoints). All rows are our runs of the MSEB evaluation code with L2-normalised audio embeddings; reasoning and retrieval are scored as in the current MSEB release (per-model no-answer threshold, exact retrieval). We report the mean over 62 higher-is-better scores from 52 tasks (speaker clustering scores age, gender and speaker identity separately), domain-specific means (40 speech, 2 environmental sound, 20 bird sound scores) and task-specific means: classification (16), clustering (26), reranking (5), reasoning (5), and retrieval (10). Stability (Stab., 5 tasks) is the continuous edit distance between the embeddings of clean and noise-augmented speech; it is lower-is-better and not part of the mean. Speech transcription and salient-term segmentation are not reported, since they score text output that an embedding model does not produce. Multilingual tasks, the image–speech retrieval tasks, and the full-index document retrieval (we use the small index) are not run. All scores are in %. The best value in each column is bolded.",1],[0,30,61,"The catalogue lists each of the 450 sources with at least 10 LAION-Audio-XL audio samples at three stages: as stored audio in the archive, as selected audio in the 500M selection, and as audio samples of at most 30 seconds in LAION-Audio-XL. The archive counts come from the collection of stored audio samples with their measured durations. The archive lists 1.44B of the 1.48B stored samples. Rows that link to the same source page are combined into one row.",0],[0,30,61,"Sources are grouped as academic datasets, internet datasets, web crawl and web collections, or YouTube-derived data. A source is academic when a citable publication was found for it: an arXiv preprint, a DOI that resolves to a paper, or a citation found on the source or derivative page. A source is an internet dataset when the same search did not find such publication, which is the usual case for a Hugging Face upload released without a paper. Websites such as Kaggle uploads count as webcrawled data unless the Kaggle page cites a paper that introduces the data, and so do uploads whose page says nothing about the data.",0],[0,30,61,"The distinction of academic dataset, internet dataset, etc. describes the evidence we could find, and a later publication can move a source between the two groups. A paper that only uses the data does not count. Only the YouTube entry forms the YouTube-derived group, as other datasets that built from YouTube but are their own publication, such as AudioSet [49] and VGGSound [114], are academic.",0],[0,30,61,"In total, about 89.0% (327.3 million) of LAION-Audio-XL contains a Creative Commons license. When counting relatively per data source, about 60% have a Creative Commons declaration: 23% CC BY, 12% CC BY-SA, 15% variants with non-commercial or no-derivatives restrictions, 5% CC0, and 4% mixed or unspecified Creative Commons terms. About 8% list software licenses such as Apache, MIT or GPL, 1.5% public-domain declarations, and 19% other or source-specific terms. The remaining 11% do not have a verified license declaration. These percentages count each source equally, without weighting by audio samples or hours. They summarize recorded declarations, which can include audio, annotations, metadata, or code. About 11% of all sources rely on inventory declarations that have not been verified on a source page.",0],[0,30,61,"Table 15: Sources, stored audio samples and indexed audio hours by group, at three stages: the archive as collected, the 500M selection (the selection index), and LAION-Audio-XL (audio samples at most 30 seconds long). A clip re-cut from a recording above 120 seconds is charged once to its source in the last two stages. The archive counts the recording as collected. Rows count the sources listed in Table 16, those with at least 10 LAION-Audio-XL audio samples. The deduplicated pool between the first two stages is given in Figure 2.",1],[0,30,61,"Table 16: Complete dataset catalogue, alphabetized inside each group. Archive counts every clip stored in the collection before deduplication. 500M selection counts the selected audio samples. LAION-Audio-XL counts the subset of audio samples of at most 30 seconds. This catalogue includes 2,723,019 recovered audio samples. A source counts as academic if it is linked to a publication, in the broadest sense. Otherwise it counts as an internet source. Only sources with at least 10 LAION-Audio-XL audio samples are listed. Another 40 sources contribute between one and nine audio samples each. Names link to source pages where the dataset is found, or in some cases the paper itself.",1]],"abbr":{"CLAP":"contrastive language-audio pretraining","CLIP":"contrastive language-image pretraining","MAEB-30":"Massive Audio Embedding Benchmark","HEAR":"Holistic Evaluation of Audio Representations benchmark","MSEB":"Massive Sound Embedding Benchmark","NIC":"Neumann Institute for Computing","JSC":"Jülich Supercomputing Centre","HDF":"Helmholtz Data Federation","ZS":"zero-shot"}};
